Subject: [PATCH] detect operand-only changes (constants, stack sizes)

ghidriff does not report a function whose only change is an operand: a
bounds constant, a stack frame size, a struct offset. The function is not
listed as modified; it does not appear in the report at all.

The 'code' diff type is gated on:

    if len(only_code_diff) > 0 and (mnemonics_ratio < 1.0 or blocks_ratio < 1.0):

`instructions` is str(code_unit) and keeps operands ("cmp x0, #0x40").
`mnemonics` is code_unit.getMnemonicString() and drops them ("cmp"). An
operand-only edit leaves both mnemonics_ratio and blocks_ratio at 1.0, so the
guard is False and 'code' is never appended. When no other diff_type fires,
the function is then discarded entirely by the `if len(diff_type) == 0:
continue` below.

`instructions_ratio` is already computed on the preceding line and is < 1.0
for precisely these cases. It is carried into the result as 'i_ratio' but
never consulted when deciding whether code changed. Adding it to the guard
closes the gap without introducing a new comparison.

This matters because an operand-only change is a normal shape for a security
fix. The canonical example is MS03-026 (Blaster), whose patch was a stack
buffer size change. Measured on a four-way harness built from one source
(scripts/ghidriff-blindspot-test.sh in the wt-678 pipeline repo):

    case     same __text size   modified_funcs_len   before -> after
    base     yes                0                    0  (control, must stay 0)
    const    yes                0                    1   n > 64 -> n > 32
    stack    yes                0                    1   buf[256] -> buf[128]
    struct   no                 1                    1   added if statement

Measured with ghidriff 1.x, Ghidra 12.2, --min-func-len 1, on arm64 macOS;
both the SimpleDiff and default engines behave the same way.

--- a/ghidriff/ghidra_diff_engine.py
+++ b/ghidriff/ghidra_diff_engine.py
@@ -1729,7 +1729,13 @@
             only_code_diff = ''.join(list(difflib.unified_diff(old_code_no_sig, new_code_no_sig, lineterm='\n',
                                      fromfile=from_file_name, tofile=to_file_name)))  # ignores name changes

-            if len(only_code_diff) > 0 and (mnemonics_ratio < 1.0 or blocks_ratio < 1.0):
+            # instructions_ratio is the only one of the three that is sensitive
+            # to operands. Without it an operand-only change — a bounds
+            # constant, a stack frame size, a struct offset — leaves mnemonics
+            # and blocks identical, so 'code' is never appended and the
+            # function is dropped by the empty-diff_type check below.
+            if len(only_code_diff) > 0 and (instructions_ratio < 1.0
+                                            or mnemonics_ratio < 1.0
+                                            or blocks_ratio < 1.0):

                 # handle bad match external match (sometimes occurs with implied matches)
                 if ratio == 0.0 and (blocks_ratio == 0.0):
